.TH SMFC\-CONFIG 1 smfc\-7.0.0
.SH NAME
.B smfc\-config
\- create and edit the configuration file of smfc.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smfc\-config
[options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B smfc-config
is a full\-screen editor for
.IR /etc/smfc/smfc.conf ,
the configuration file of
.BR smfc (1).
It switches fan controllers on and off, edits every parameter, checks whether
the hardware each controller needs is reachable, and picks disks from a list
instead of asking you to type a
.I /dev/disk/by\-id/
path by hand.
.PP
A value being typed sits in a field of its own colour, with the cursor where
the next character lands. A value longer than its field scrolls with the
cursor.
.PP
It keeps the comments of your file. Only the value you change is rewritten;
every other byte stays where it was, so the documentation in the file survives
an edit.
.PP
The screen has four parts:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Sections
\(em the fan controller families on the left, with their state and the result
of the last check.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Parameters
\(em the parameters of the selected section on the right, with the value the
file holds or the default the daemon would apply.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Help
\(em what the selected parameter does, when to change it, and what it accepts.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Status and keys
\(em the state of the file and the keys that are available.
.PP
The section list always shows the same nine families, whether or not the file
holds them. A section that is missing and a section with
.B enabled=no
mean the same thing to
.BR smfc (1),
so the editor does not show a difference the daemon cannot see. Switching a
family on writes the section; switching it off writes
.B enabled=0
and keeps every value you typed.
.PP
A family may hold several instances, for example
.B [CPU]
and
.BR [CPU:1] ,
each driving a different IPMI zone with a different fan curve. Each instance is
a row of its own.
.PP
.B smfc-config
opens a file the daemon rejects. That is the case it exists for: the shipped
configuration file is invalid until the installer fills the disks in, and a
machine where that detection failed has a file
.BR smfc (1)
will not start. The parse error is shown on the section it belongs to, and you
can edit your way out of it.
.PP
Nothing blocks a save. You may leave a file half finished and come back to it.
The status line then says that
.BR smfc (1)
will not start with the file, that the running service keeps its current
settings, and that the next boot is where the fault shows.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show a help message and exit.
.TP
.BI \-c " FILE" ", \-\-config " FILE
Configuration file to edit (default:
.IR /etc/smfc/smfc.conf ).
.TP
.B \-nc, \-\-no\-color
Plain output, no colors.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-version
Show the program version and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Tab
Move between the section list and the parameter list.
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move the cursor. The letters
.B k
and
.B j
do the same.
.TP
.B Enter
In the section list, move to the parameters. In the parameter list, edit the
value: a number is typed in place, a value with two states is switched, and a
path, a fan curve or a device list opens a window. That window holds the field
alone, because the help pane stays visible below it. An empty field offers a
value: the default
.BR smfc (1)
applies when the parameter is not set, or an example where the parameter has no
default.
.B End
takes that value into the field, where it can be edited rather than typed out.
.TP
.B Space
Switch the selected family on or off.
.TP
.B F5
Run the checks, including the ones that talk to the hardware. In the card list
of
.B gpu_device_ids=
or
.BR npu_device_ids= ,
ask the vendor tool which cards the machine has:
.BR nvidia-smi ,
.B rocm-smi
or
.BR npu-smi .
.TP
.B F2
Save the file.
.TP
.B +, F6
Add an instance to a family, on an IPMI zone no enabled instance claims.
.TP
.B \-, F8
Remove the selected instance, after a confirmation.
.TP
.B Left, Right, Home, End
Move inside the value being typed.
.B End
on an empty field takes the value the field offers.
.TP
.B Backspace, Delete
Remove the character before the cursor, or the one under it.
.TP
.B Insert
Switch the field between insert and overwrite. A bar cursor inserts and pushes
the rest of the value along, a block cursor types over it. The shape
needs a terminal that can change it, which
.BR xterm (1)
and
.BR tmux (1)
can and the Linux text console cannot.
.TP
.B Esc
Leave the parameter list, or cancel what is being typed.
.TP
.B F10, q
Quit.
.SH CHECKS
The checks run when you press
.BR F5 ,
not while you type, because some of them talk to the hardware and take seconds.
They run as the user who started the editor.
.BR ipmitool (1)
and
.BR smartctl (8)
usually need root, so run the editor with
.BR sudo (8)
if a check reports a permission error.
Reading a sleeping disk with
.BR smartctl (8)
wakes it, so the editor never does that on its own.
.PP
A finding is one of three kinds.
.BR smfc (1)
will not start on an
.BR error ;
it will start on a
.BR warning ,
and the result is probably not what you meant; an
.B information
line is an observation.
.PP
A value already in the file that the daemon would reject is marked red and
explained, even before you press
.BR F5 .
The editor checks a value with the same functions the daemon uses, so a value
it accepts is a value the daemon accepts.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/smfc/smfc.conf
The configuration file.
.TP
.I /etc/smfc/smfc.conf.YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS
A backup written by the installation script. The editor offers it when the
configuration file is missing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful exit.
.TP
.B 6
The configuration file could not be opened.
.SH NOTES
Writing
.I /etc/smfc/smfc.conf
needs root rights, so run the editor with
.BR sudo (8).
Without them it opens read\-only: you can read every section and every help
text, and the status line says so at the start rather than at the first save.
.PP
The editor never restarts the service on its own. After a save it offers a
restart when the file is valid, the service is running, and it has the rights
to do it. Otherwise it prints the command for you to run.
.PP
The editor needs 64 columns and 16 rows for its two panes. Below that it falls
back to a single pane rather than refusing to start.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR smfc (1),
.BR smfc\-client (1),
.BR ipmitool (1),
.BR smartctl (8),
.BR systemctl (1)
